"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 5.0. Countervailing factors include the biopsy showing a higher Gleason grade (4+4, ISUP grade group 4) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4, ISUP grade group 2). The nodal status was documented as no lymph node metastasis. The uncertainty in the prognosis is widened by the preoperative PSA and the absence of postoperative PSA or follow-up data, which would be crucial for trajectory assessment. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 67.81–101.71 months; its coverage has not been validated."
